    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nJSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is an open, text-based data interchange format standardized by RFC 8259 and ECMA-404. Built upon a collection of key/value pairs (objects) and ordered lists of values (arrays), JSON is the universal serialization mechanism for REST APIs, GraphQL endpoints, microservices architectures, NoSQL document databases (like MongoDB and CouchDB), and application configurations. The JSON grammar strictly defines six primary data types: strings (wrapped in double quotes), numbers (integer or floating-point adhering to IEEE 754), booleans (true or false), null, arrays, and objects. Unlike JavaScript object literals, JSON enforces uncompromising syntax constraints: keys must be enclosed in double quotation marks, trailing commas are strictly forbidden after the final entry in an object or array, and character sequences must be UTF-8 encoded with valid unicode escape sequences.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's JSON Formatter executes 100% locally within your client browser using the V8 / SpiderMonkey JavaScript runtime. When you paste raw or minified JSON text, the parser leverages the native window.JSON.parse() method. During parsing, lexical tokens are evaluated, and syntax errors are captured with exact line and character column offsets. The formatter then invokes a specialized serialization pipeline—analogous to window.JSON.stringify(payload, null, 2)—that walks the AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) to calculate depth levels, inject proportional indentation, and color-code structural delimiters ({}, []), property keys, numeric scalars, and string values using syntax-highlighted DOM tokens.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nWhen working with JSON across full-stack applications, developers regularly encounter subtle syntax traps that cause runtime parser crashes:\n- Single quotes: Using single quotes ('key': 'value') instead of double quotes (\"key\": \"value\") is the #1 syntax error. JSON strictly rejects single quotation marks.\n- Trailing commas: Placing a comma after the final object property or array element (e.g. {\"a\": 1,}) violates RFC 8259, crashing strict parsers in Go, Python, Java, and C#.\n- Unquoted property names: Omitting quotes around object keys is valid JavaScript, but completely invalid JSON.\n- Numeric precision loss: Because JSON numbers map to IEEE 754 double-precision floats in JavaScript, 64-bit integers exceeding 2^53 - 1 (9,007,199,254,740,991) lose precision unless represented as strings.\n- Unescaped control characters: Raw newline characters and carriage returns within string literals must be escaped as \\n and \\r.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nModern applications exchange highly sensitive information over JSON: API tokens, bearer authorization headers, OAuth credentials, user PII (Personally Identifiable Information), and enterprise database exports. Unlike server-side formatting websites that log your payloads to backend servers, WebUtil operates under a strict zero-knowledge architecture. All parsing, validation, pretty-printing, and minification occur exclusively in your browser's local sandbox memory. Zero network requests are initiated, zero analytics inspect your payload, and zero bytes are stored on external disks. You can format private production data with total confidence.",
