    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nHTML Entity encoding is a critical mechanism standardized by the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) in the HTML5 specification and XML 1.0 recommendations. In HyperText Markup Language, certain characters are reserved as structural markup delimiters: the less-than sign '<' opens a tag, the greater-than sign '>' closes a tag, the ampersand '&' initiates a character reference, and quotation marks '\"' and '\\'' delimit HTML attributes. When user-supplied text contains these reserved characters without encoding, web browsers interpret them as active markup rather than literal text. HTML entities provide named character references (such as &amp;, &lt;, &gt;, &quot;, &apos;) and numeric character references (NCRs in decimal like &#60; or hexadecimal like &#x3C;) that instruct the browser's DOM parser to render the glyph visually without executing it as code.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's HTML Entities tool processes your content locally using the browser's internal DOM parsing engine. For encoding, special characters are mapped to their standardized W3C entity representations using optimized regular expression lookups and character code mappings. For decoding, the tool creates an in-memory DOM text node inside an isolated DocumentFragment, reading back the deserialized textContent. This approach guarantees that even nested, legacy, or obscure HTML5 named entities (such as &copy;, &euro;, &trade;, and math symbols) are decoded with 100% fidelity matching official browser behavior.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nCorrect HTML entity escaping is the foundational layer of web application security:\n- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention: Unescaped user input rendered directly into an HTML template allows attackers to inject malicious <script> tags or inline event handlers (onload, onerror). Escaping '<' as &lt; and '>' as &gt; prevents the browser from constructing rogue DOM elements.\n- Context-dependent escaping: Escaping rules vary by context. Escaping inside an HTML body requires converting &, <, and >. Escaping inside an HTML attribute (e.g. value=\"...\") additionally requires escaping double and single quotes. Escaping inside a <script> block requires JavaScript escaping, where HTML entities will not execute properly.\n- Double-escaping bug: When an already-escaped string like &amp; is escaped again, it turns into &amp;amp;. This is a frequent bug in CMS platforms and content pipelines.\n- Modern Unicode: With universal UTF-8 support across modern web browsers, named entities for international characters (like &eacute;) are no longer required; literal Unicode characters can be used directly, reserving entities primarily for syntax characters (&, <, >, \", ').\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nDevelopers and security engineers frequently inspect proprietary code snippets, customer feedback strings, and vulnerability payloads when testing entity encoding. WebUtil operates strictly client-side. No HTML strings or sanitization payloads are transmitted over the internet or logged to any database.\n\n## Content Management & Internationalization\nModern web applications exchange text content across multiple international character sets and localized databases. Converting special characters to standardized HTML entities guarantees consistent rendering across older email clients, legacy web platforms, and diverse rendering engines that may lack full UTF-8 font support, preventing character corruption (mojibake) and display errors.",
    faqItems: [
      { question: "What are HTML entities and why are they used?", answer: "HTML entities are special character codes used to represent reserved HTML characters (like < and >) and typographic symbols so browsers render them as visual text rather than executable markup." },
      { question: "How does HTML entity encoding prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)?", answer: "When user input containing characters like <script> is converted to &lt;script&gt;, the browser renders the code harmlessly as text on the screen rather than executing it as JavaScript in the DOM." },
      { question: "What are the 5 critical HTML characters that must always be escaped?", answer: "The five essential characters are: & (becomes &amp;), < (becomes &lt;), > (becomes &gt;), \" (becomes &quot;), and ' (becomes &#39; or &apos;)." },
      { question: "What is the difference between named entities and numeric character references?", answer: "Named entities use readable names (like &copy; or &euro;), while numeric character references use decimal (&#169;) or hexadecimal (&#xA9;) Unicode code points. Numeric references work for every Unicode glyph." },
      { question: "When should you encode vs decode HTML entities in web development?", answer: "Encode text before rendering dynamic, untrusted user data into HTML templates. Decode entities when parsing scraped HTML or converting HTML document content back into plain text." }
    ],
