    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nDigital raster image formats represent visual data using distinct compression algorithms, color depths, and structural standards defined by the ISO, IEC, and W3C:\n- PNG (Portable Network Graphics): Standardized by ISO/IEC 15948. A lossless format utilizing DEFLATE compression with full 8-bit alpha transparency support. Ideal for diagrams, UI icons, screenshots, and graphics requiring sharp edges.\n- JPEG (Joint Photographic Experts Group): Standardized by ISO/IEC 10918-1. A lossy format utilizing Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) compression. Ideal for continuous-tone photography, offering dramatic file size reduction at the expense of slight compression artifacts.\n- WebP: A modern image format developed by Google based on the VP8 video codec standard. WebP supports both lossy and lossless compression, alpha transparency, and animated frames, typically producing files 25% to 35% smaller than comparable JPEGs or PNGs.\n- BMP & GIF: Legacy formats representing uncompressed bitmaps and 8-bit indexed palette animations.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nUnlike conventional online image converters that upload your photos to a remote cloud server, WebUtil performs 100% of image decoding, processing, and re-encoding locally in your browser using the HTML5 <canvas> element and modern browser Graphics Processing APIs. When you select or drop an image, the browser loads the binary file into an HTMLImageElement or ImageBitmap via URL.createObjectURL(). The pixels are drawn onto an offscreen canvas matching the source dimensions. WebUtil then calls canvas.toBlob() with your requested format (image/webp, image/jpeg, or image/png) and quality factor (0.0 to 1.0), triggering the browser's native C++ image codec to output a fresh, optimized binary blob for instant download.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nChoosing the right image format and conversion settings is crucial for web performance and Core Web Vitals:\n- Next-Gen Formats for Web Performance: Converting legacy PNGs and JPEGs to WebP significantly reduces Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) times on modern websites, saving mobile bandwidth without perceptible visual degradation.\n- Preserving Alpha Transparency: Converting a transparent PNG to JPEG replaces transparent background areas with black or white pixels because the JPEG format does not support alpha channels. Convert to WebP or retain PNG to preserve transparency.\n- Lossy Quality Thresholds: For WebP and JPEG, setting a quality level between 0.80 and 0.85 achieves optimal visual fidelity with dramatic file size savings. Dropping below 0.70 introduces visible block artifacts.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nPhotos often contain highly personal imagery, proprietary design mockups, financial documents, or confidential identification cards. WebUtil's image converter runs entirely in your local browser sandbox. Your images are never uploaded to any remote server or stored in the cloud.\n\n## Code Audit & Regulatory Compliance\nComparing configuration files and source code revisions is an essential audit requirement for compliance frameworks like SOC 2, HIPAA, and ISO 27001. Performing difference evaluations locally in the browser provides software teams with an immediate, private method to audit environmental changes, examine database schema diffs, and inspect release candidates without exposing proprietary algorithms or credentials to third-party web services.\n\n## Algorithmic Complexity & Memory Efficiency\nBy maintaining an internal matrix buffer and utilizing heuristic pruning, modern diff implementations achieve near-linear memory consumption O(ND) on typical source code changes. This efficiency ensures that comparing multi-megabyte JSON payloads, SQL dumps, or localized translation files executes instantaneously without taxing system CPU resources.",
