    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nJSON Web Token (JWT) is an open, compact, and URL-safe industry standard for securely representing claims between two parties, defined by RFC 7519. Widely utilized in modern web authentication, Single Sign-On (SSO), and microservice authorization architectures (OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect), a JWT is serialized as a string consisting of three distinct base64url-encoded JSON objects separated by periods (.):\n- Header: Specifies the token type ('typ': 'JWT') and the cryptographic signing algorithm ('alg', such as HS256 for HMAC-SHA256, RS256 for RSA, or ES256 for ECDSA), standardized by RFC 7515 (JSON Web Signature).\n- Payload: Contains the 'claims'—statements about an entity (typically the user) and additional metadata. RFC 7519 defines standard registered claims: 'iss' (issuer), 'sub' (subject/user ID), 'aud' (audience), 'exp' (expiration timestamp), 'nbf' (not before), 'iat' (issued at), and 'jti' (JWT ID), alongside application-specific custom claims.\n- Signature: Computed by hashing the encoded header and payload with a secret key or private key to ensure message integrity.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's JWT Decoder operates completely inside your client browser. When a JWT string is supplied, the tool splits the string by period delimiters. The header and payload segments are decoded from Base64URL encoding into raw UTF-8 JSON strings. The tool formats the JSON payloads with syntax highlighting, parses standard registered claims, converts Unix epoch timestamps (such as 'exp' and 'iat') into human-readable UTC and local date formats, and evaluates whether the token is currently valid or expired based on your computer's local clock.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nDecoding and managing JWTs in web applications requires strict adherence to security protocols:\n- Decoding vs Verifying: Decoding a JWT simply reveals the data inside the header and payload. It DOES NOT prove the token was issued by a trusted server or that it has not been tampered with. Only cryptographic signature verification using the issuer's public key or shared secret proves authenticity.\n- The 'alg': 'none' Vulnerability: In early JWT implementations, attackers could alter the header to set 'alg': 'none' and strip the signature, tricking naive backends into accepting unsigned tokens. Modern libraries strictly forbid this.\n- Sensitive Data in Payloads: JWT payloads are encoded, NOT encrypted (unless using JSON Web Encryption, RFC 7516). Anyone who inspects the token can read all claims. Never place passwords, credit card numbers, or sensitive secrets in a JWT payload.\n- Storing Tokens in Web Browsers: Storing JWTs in localStorage exposes them to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) theft. Storing them in HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies is the security standard for web sessions.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nAuthentication tokens contain live session permissions, user IDs, email addresses, and corporate roles. Pasting live JWTs into untrusted online decoders risks leaking credentials to third parties. WebUtil decodes JWTs 100% locally in your browser tab. Your tokens never leave your device.\n\n## Enterprise Protocol Diagnostics & Schema Validation\nEnterprise integration protocols like SOAP web services, Electronic Data Interchange (EDI), and healthcare HL7 XML feeds rely on strictly validated structures. When troubleshooting integration failures, minified XML payload strings obscure namespace scope mismatches and unclosed tags. Beautifying XML with syntax indentation accelerates debugging, facilitates schema validation against XSD files, and prevents XML External Entity (XXE) vulnerabilities during system integration.",
    faqItems: [
      { question: "Can a JWT be decoded without knowing the secret signature key?", answer: "Yes. Standard JWTs (JWS) are signed, not encrypted. The header and payload are Base64URL-encoded JSON strings that anyone can decode and read without the signing secret." },
      { question: "What is the difference between a JWT header, payload, and signature?", answer: "The Header specifies metadata and the signing algorithm (e.g. RS256). The Payload contains the claims and data (user ID, expiration, roles). The Signature cryptographically verifies that the token was not modified after issuance." },
      { question: "What are standard JWT registered claims (iss, sub, aud, exp, iat)?", answer: "Standard claims include: iss (issuer), sub (subject/user ID), aud (audience/intended recipient), exp (expiration timestamp), nbf (not before timestamp), and iat (issued-at timestamp)." },
      { question: "What is the 'alg: none' JWT vulnerability?", answer: "It is a severe security vulnerability where flawed authentication libraries accept tokens that specify 'none' as their algorithm, bypassing signature verification entirely." },
      { question: "Is it safe to paste production authentication tokens into WebUtil?", answer: "Yes. WebUtil runs 100% client-side in your browser. No tokens, claims, or telemetry are ever sent across the network." },
      { question: "Where should JWT tokens be stored securely in web applications?", answer: "For web applications, storing JWTs in httpOnly, secure, sameSite cookies protects them from XSS attacks. Avoid storing sensitive tokens in localStorage or sessionStorage." }
    ],
