    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nCryptographic hash functions are mathematical algorithms that transform arbitrary-length input data into a fixed-size bit string (message digest), standardized by NIST (FIPS PUB 180-4 for Secure Hash Standard) and the IETF:\n- SHA-256 and SHA-512 (SHA-2 Family): Industry-standard 256-bit and 512-bit secure hash algorithms required by TLS, Bitcoin blockchain, digital certificates, and modern operating systems.\n- SHA-384 and SHA-224: Truncated variants of the SHA-2 family tailored for specific key exchange protocols.\n- SHA-1: 160-bit algorithm defined in RFC 3174. Considered deprecated for security-sensitive applications due to practical collision attacks demonstrated in 2017 (SHAttered), but still widely used for Git commit hashing and legacy checksums.\n- MD5 (Message Digest 5): 128-bit algorithm defined in RFC 1321. Deprecated for cryptographic signatures due to collision vulnerabilities, but extensively used for non-cryptographic checksum verification and database cache keys.\nFundamental cryptographic properties include preimage resistance (one-way property), second preimage resistance (weak collision resistance), and the avalanche effect (a single bit change flips approximately 50% of output bits).\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's Hash Generator leverages the native Web Cryptography API (window.crypto.subtle.digest()) built into modern browser engines. Input strings are encoded into binary UTF-8 octets via TextEncoder and processed directly by compiled C++ cryptographic routines in your browser's security sandbox. For MD5—which is omitted from the Web Crypto standard due to legacy status—the tool utilizes an optimized, pure-JavaScript implementation. Digests are formatted as lowercase and uppercase hexadecimal strings and Base64-encoded strings for immediate use in terminal commands and configuration files.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nHashing is foundational to software engineering, yet frequently misapplied:\n- Hashing vs Encryption: Hashing is a one-way, irreversible transformation; encryption is a two-way transformation designed to be decrypted with a secret key. Never attempt to 'decrypt' a SHA-256 hash.\n- Password Storage Antipattern: Fast cryptographic hash functions like SHA-256 or MD5 must NEVER be used alone for password storage because modern GPU clusters can compute billions of SHA-256 hashes per second. Passwords require slow, memory-hard algorithms such as Argon2id or bcrypt.\n- File Integrity Checksums: When downloading ISOs, binaries, or release packages, comparing the SHA-256 checksum of your downloaded file against the publisher's published digest guarantees the file was not corrupted in transit or tampered with by a man-in-the-middle attacker.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nDevelopers regularly hash proprietary source code, internal file paths, API secrets, and sensitive tokens. WebUtil executes all hashing locally on your machine. Your input strings and resulting hashes never leave your device.\n\n## Security Best Practices & Checksum Verification\nWhen distributing open-source software, compiled binaries, or container images, publishing cryptographic digests (such as SHA-256 or SHA-512) enables downstream users to verify file integrity. Comparing calculated hashes against vendor checksums guarantees that downloaded files have not been corrupted during network transit or tampered with by hostile actors in supply chain compromise attacks.",
    faqItems: [
      { question: "What is a cryptographic hash function and how does it work?", answer: "A cryptographic hash function takes any text or binary input and processes it through mathematical compression functions to produce a fixed-length string (digest) that cannot be reversed." },
      { question: "Can a SHA-256 or SHA-512 hash be decrypted or reversed?", answer: "No. Cryptographic hashes are one-way mathematical operations, not encryption. They contain no decryption key. The only way to find the original text is guessing (brute force or dictionary attacks)." },
      { question: "Why are MD5 and SHA-1 no longer considered secure?", answer: "Researchers have discovered collision vulnerabilities in both MD5 and SHA-1, meaning attackers can generate two different files that produce the exact same hash. Use SHA-256 or SHA-512 for security." },
      { question: "What is the difference between hashing and encryption?", answer: "Encryption is a two-way function designed to hide data with a secret key so it can later be decrypted. Hashing is a one-way function designed to verify data integrity and identity without revealing original contents." },
      { question: "Why should I use Bcrypt or Argon2 instead of SHA-256 for user passwords?", answer: "SHA-256 is designed to be fast, enabling attackers to test billions of password guesses per second on GPUs. Password hashing algorithms like Argon2 and Bcrypt are deliberately slow and memory-hard to prevent brute-force attacks." },
      { question: "How does WebUtil compute hashes in the browser?", answer: "WebUtil uses the browser's native window.crypto.subtle.digest API, executing cryptographic math on your local CPU without sending any data over the network." }
    ],
