    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nJWT (JSON Web Token) lifetime and validity are governed by RFC 7519 Registered Claim Names. A secure authentication or authorization token incorporates three temporal claims:\n- exp (Expiration Time): A Unix epoch timestamp (seconds since January 1, 1970, 00:00:00 UTC) identifying the exact moment after which the token MUST NOT be accepted for processing.\n- nbf (Not Before): A Unix epoch timestamp identifying the time before which the token MUST NOT be accepted.\n- iat (Issued At): A Unix epoch timestamp recording the creation time of the token.\nIn distributed microservices, network latency and minor server clock drift require token validation systems to implement a small clock skew tolerance (typically 30 seconds to 5 minutes) when evaluating whether currentTime >= exp.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's JWT Expiration Checker runs entirely within your browser tab. Upon entering a token, the tool extracts the payload segment, base64url-decodes it into JSON, and extracts the 'exp', 'iat', and 'nbf' timestamps. It compares these timestamps against your computer's local clock (Date.now() / 1000). The tool visualizes the exact countdown time remaining until expiration (days, hours, minutes, seconds), provides ISO 8601 UTC and local timestamps, and flags whether the token is currently active, expired, or not yet valid.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nManaging token lifetimes is a cornerstone of modern web security and session management:\n- Short-Lived Access Tokens: Access tokens should have short lifetimes—typically 5 to 15 minutes. If an access token is intercepted or leaked, an attacker's window of opportunity is limited to the remaining expiration window.\n- Token Rotation with Refresh Tokens: Long-lived sessions should be maintained using secure refresh tokens stored in HttpOnly cookies, exchanged for fresh short-lived access tokens via token rotation endpoints.\n- Revocation Limitations: Because JWTs are stateless, a backend cannot trivially revoke a valid, unexpired JWT without maintaining an active token blacklist or revoking the user's signing key. Enforcing tight 'exp' boundaries minimizes exposure.\n- Client vs Server Clock Discrepancies: If a client device has an inaccurate clock (e.g. clock set 10 minutes fast), local expiration checks may report a token as expired even though the backend server still accepts it. Always synchronize servers with NTP (Network Time Protocol).\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nLive authentication tokens carry valid session access. Pasting active tokens into third-party servers could allow rogue operators to hijack your accounts. WebUtil checks token expiration completely in your browser's local sandbox memory. Zero token bytes are transmitted over the network.\n\n## Stateless Session Architecture & Security\nStateless authentication models powered by JSON Web Tokens eliminate centralized session database lookups across distributed microservices. However, managing token lifespan requires balancing user convenience against security exposure. Implementing brief token validity windows (5–15 minutes) coupled with automated background refresh token rotation provides an optimal architecture that minimizes session theft risk while maintaining seamless user authentication.",
    faqItems: [
      { question: "How is expiration time (exp) stored inside a JSON Web Token?", answer: "The 'exp' claim is stored as a NumericDate: a Unix epoch timestamp representing the number of seconds since January 1, 1970 UTC." },
      { question: "What is clock skew and why do authentication servers allow tolerance?", answer: "Clock skew refers to slight time differences between servers. Auth libraries typically allow a 30-60 second margin when checking 'exp' and 'nbf' to prevent valid tokens from being rejected due to server time drift." },
      { question: "What is the difference between exp, nbf, and iat claims in a JWT?", answer: "'exp' defines when the token expires, 'nbf' defines the earliest time the token can be accepted, and 'iat' records the exact time the token was issued." },
      { question: "How should single-page applications handle expired JWTs?", answer: "SPAs should track token expiration and request a fresh access token using a secure refresh token before the current access token expires, preventing user session interruptions." },
      { question: "Is checking JWT expiration in the browser secure without server verification?", answer: "Client-side expiration checks are great for UI session timers, but production APIs must always independently verify the cryptographic signature and expiration on the backend." }
    ],
