    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nWi-Fi QR Codes encode local wireless network connection parameters into a standardized string format recognized natively by iOS (Camera app), Android (Google Lens / Wi-Fi Settings), and modern mobile operating systems. The standardized Wi-Fi configuration URI scheme follows the syntax:\nWIFI:S:<SSID>;T:<WPA|WEP|nopass>;P:<password>;H:<true|false>;;\n- S: The Service Set Identifier (SSID)—the human-readable broadcast name of the Wi-Fi network.\n- T: The authentication type, typically 'WPA' (covering WPA, WPA2-PSK, and WPA3-SAE personal networks), 'WEP' (legacy Wired Equivalent Privacy), or 'nopass' for open networks.\n- P: The pre-shared key (Wi-Fi password). Special characters (;, :, \\, \") are escaped with backslashes per spec.\n- H: Optional boolean flag ('true' or 'false') indicating whether the network SSID is hidden (not broadcasting beacon frames).\nThe resulting URI string is converted into a matrix barcode conforming to ISO/IEC 18004.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's Wi-Fi QR Code Generator formats the network credentials into the standardized WIFI:... syntax and feeds the string into a client-side QR generation engine. The engine calculates the optimal QR Code version (size from 1 to 40) based on string length and applies Reed-Solomon algebraic error correction (Level M or H). The barcode matrix is rendered directly into an HTML5 <canvas> or Scalable Vector Graphic (<svg>), allowing instant on-screen scanning or high-resolution downloading for physical printout on office cards, guest tables, or router stickers.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nGenerating Wi-Fi QR codes eliminates human friction while maintaining network security:\n- Special Character Escaping: Wi-Fi passwords frequently contain semicolons (;), colons (:), and backslashes (\\). In the WIFI: URI format, these characters must be escaped with a preceding backslash (e.g. \\; or \\:), otherwise mobile scanners parse the URI incorrectly. WebUtil automatically handles escaping.\n- Hidden SSIDs: If your router is configured not to broadcast its SSID, the H:true parameter is mandatory. Without it, scanning devices will fail to locate and associate with the hidden access point.\n- Error Correction for Print: If you plan to print Wi-Fi QR codes on laminated tent cards or stickers, use high error correction (Level Q or H). This allows the code to be read even if the printed surface suffers scratches, smudges, or low lighting.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nYour Wi-Fi password grants physical network access to your home or office local devices (smart TVs, NAS drives, IoT cameras, internal dev servers). Using third-party online QR generators risks transmitting your network credentials and SSID to external databases. WebUtil generates all Wi-Fi QR codes 100% locally in your browser. Zero network credentials ever touch a server.\n\n## Physical Verification & Print Alignment\nBefore printing Wi-Fi QR codes on physical signage, verify that camera sensors can resolve the code under diverse angles and glare conditions. Providing high error-correction margins ensures codes remain effortlessly scannable for guests, employees, and customers across both iOS and Android camera apps.",
