    content: "## Technical Specifications & Standards\nExchangeable Image File Format (EXIF) is the international standard for storing metadata within digital photography files, standardized by the Japan Electronics and Information Technology Industries Association (JEITA) under specification CP-3451 and commonly accompanied by IPTC-NAA and Adobe XMP (Extensible Metadata Platform) metadata blocks. When a photograph is captured using a modern smartphone (iPhone or Android) or digital camera, the device automatically embeds dozens of metadata tags into the JPEG header (within the APP1 marker segment) or TIFF IFD (Image File Directory) structures:\n- GPS Metadata: GPS Latitude, Longitude, Altitude, Timestamp, and Direction accurate to within a few meters.\n- Device Identification: Camera make, camera model, lens serial number, and firmware version.\n- Capture Parameters: Shutter speed, aperture (F-number), ISO sensitivity, focal length, metering mode, and flash firing status.\n- Temporal & Personal Data: Exact creation timestamp, owner name, copyright notice, and embedded thumbnail images.\n\n## How It Works in Your Browser\nWebUtil's Photo EXIF & GPS Location Stripper provides a two-in-one privacy inspection and sanitization engine that runs 100% client-side:\n1. Inspection Phase: The tool reads the file's binary stream via FileReader, searches for the JPEG APP1 marker (0xFFE1) and EXIF header ('Exif\\0\\0'), decodes TIFF IFD tags and GPS rational values, and displays embedded camera settings and geographic coordinates.\n2. Sanitization Phase: To strip 100% of metadata with absolute mathematical certainty, the image is rendered onto an offscreen HTML5 <canvas> element matching the photo's native pixel resolution. The canvas image data is then re-encoded into a fresh JPEG/PNG container via canvas.toBlob() at 95% quality. Because the canvas API only reads and renders raw RGB pixel values, all non-pixel metadata headers (EXIF, GPS, IPTC, XMP, thumbnails) are left behind, producing a completely sanitized photo.\n\n## Developer Guide & Common Pitfalls\nUnderstanding image metadata is essential for personal privacy and web application security:\n- The Privacy Threat of GPS Location in Photos: Taking photos at your home, child's school, or workplace and uploading them to classified sites (Craigslist, Facebook Marketplace), online forums, or personal blogs allows anyone to download the image, inspect the EXIF GPS tags, and pinpoint your exact physical address on a map.\n- Image Orientation Tags (EXIF Orientation): Smartphone cameras often save photos upside down or sideways, storing an EXIF Orientation tag (values 1–8) that tells the viewer how to rotate the photo. Modern browsers automatically respect orientation when rendering to canvas, ensuring your sanitized photo remains correctly oriented.\n- Retaining Pixel Resolution: Some naive sanitizers downscale photos during stripping. WebUtil preserves your image's exact original width and height, maintaining full photographic quality.\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Privacy & Security Assurance\nYour personal photos deserve uncompromising privacy protection. WebUtil inspects and strips EXIF metadata entirely within your browser tab. Zero photos, location coordinates, or metadata tags are ever uploaded to any server.",
